#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Check orca-kit:spec files against the rules for the status they claim.

Usage:
  check [--as STATUS] [--repo ROOT] <spec.md> [<spec.md> ...]
  check --section HEADING <spec.md>
  check --rules

  --as STATUS   Check the content as if the spec claimed STATUS (idea, draft or
                accepted). Refine uses `--as accepted` on a draft before asking
                the user to accept it. The accepted_by/accepted_on rules are
                skipped, because only the user's yes can satisfy them.
  --repo ROOT   Also check the spec against the target repo at ROOT: linked
                files and symbols exist, npm/yarn/pnpm/bun scripts, make
                targets and just recipes exist, and an accepted spec isn't
                older than the code it links to. These are warnings only.
  --section HEADING
                Print one `## HEADING` section of the spec, and exit. Agents
                use it to load one section instead of the whole file.
  --rules       List the rules checked at each status, and exit.

Prints one line per problem:
  <file>:<line>: <error|warning> [<rule>] <section>: <problem>. Fix: <what to do>.
and `<file>: ok (<status>)` for a file with no errors.

Exit codes: 0 every file passes, 1 a rule fails, 2 a usage or parse error.
Warnings never change the exit code.

Python 3 standard library only; no install step. `--repo` also uses git for
its staleness check, and skips that check if git or the repo isn't there.
"""

